<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>History of Culture</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">From traits to contexts.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early anthropology, most clearly in Boas’ cultural relativism, rejected universal rankings of peoples and argued that cultural phenomena must be read in their historical and local contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boas, 1911</a>)</cite>. This moved the field away from trait lists to contextual explanations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretive turn.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Geertz recast culture as ’webs of significance’, urging a thick description of symbols, rituals, and everyday acts to understand meaningmaking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geertz, 1973</a>)</cite>. This positioned interpretation, not only measurement, at the center of cultural analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Writing culture.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The “writing culture” turn highlighted that accounts of culture are constructed through the researcher’s situated narrative, not simply discovered “out there” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clifford and Marcus, 1986</a>)</cite>. Reflexivity and positionality became methodological commitments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Performance and interaction.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Goffman’s lens treated culture as enacted through roles, audience design, and situational constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goffman, 1959</a>)</cite>. Cultural competence is thus interactional and contingent, a view that motivates our attention to context sensitive evaluation in the main text.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Taxonomy Details and Derivation</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix explains how we derived, applied, and validated the four-part taxonomy for cultural evaluation in NLP. The goal is to make our coding process transparent while keeping the main text focused. We describe the corpus and versioning we analyzed, the coder workflow and decision rules we used, and the operational cues that anchored each lens.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Derivation Procedure</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compiled a corpus of 20 cultural benchmarks and closely related resources that are widely cited in recent NLP work. For each resource, we recorded version identifiers, access dates, and any published annotation or scoring guidelines. Two coders independently reviewed task definitions, item formats, instructions to annotators, and evaluation metrics. Each coder proposed lens assignments with short rationales that referenced concrete aspects of item design or scoring. We then held an adjudication pass to resolve disagreements, documented the rationale for the final labels, and logged any unresolved ambiguities for follow-up sensitivity checks. This procedure emphasized stable signals in the written protocols rather than impressions from a small number of example items.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Coding Scheme and Decision Rules</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each benchmark received a primary lens that reflected the central evaluation target and, when warranted, a secondary lens to acknowledge additional design dependencies. We allowed multiple lenses only when both the item format and the scoring rubric would be materially altered by removing that dimension. When knowledge and preference co-occurred, the use of acceptability ratings, Likert scales, or aggregation to a group label was taken as evidence that preference should be primary. When a benchmark scored role-sensitive behavior, register selection, mitigation, or adaptation to context, we labeled dynamics as primary. Bias was labeled primary when the stated goal was to measure harm, disparity, or stereotype reproduction across groups, regardless of whether cultural facts or moral ratings appeared as supporting context. All decisions were based on observable artifacts, such as prompt templates, instructions, and metric definitions, rather than inferences about intended use.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Operational Cues for Each Lens</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Culture-as-knowledge is indicated when items require factual recall or recognition tied to cultural artifacts, calendars, practices, or named entities that can be verified against grounded sources. Typical scoring relies on exact match, span match, or objective keying, and correctness does not depend on the social situation in which the answer is given.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Culture-as-preference is indicated when items elicit attitudes, norms, or moral judgments and when evaluation aggregates individual responses into a shared profile. Common signals include Likert style ratings, pairwise moral or social acceptability judgments, and scoring keyed to alignment with an aggregate label rather than to a single verifiable fact. Explanations may be collected, but the metric treats the aggregate judgment as the target.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Culture-as-dynamics is indicated when items are situated scenes or dialogues where roles, relationships, stakes, and settings shape what counts as appropriate language use. Here, success depends on interactional choices such as register selection, mitigation strategies, face management, and norm negotiation. Scoring rubrics explicitly reference contextual appropriateness or turn design, rather than only the final outcome.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Culture-as-bias is indicated when the central objective of the benchmark is to measure harms or disparities related to identity. Typical signals include prompts designed to surface stereotype triggers, metrics that report group gaps or bias directionality, and protocols that treat harm detection and reduction as the primary objective. The presence of cultural facts or moral ratings in such datasets serves context but does not redefine the evaluation target.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Description of Reviewed Benchmarks</h2>

